The LPF's stopband can introduce stopband ripples, which may cause residual interference. This residual interference can affect the overall performance and capacity of the system. To optimize the LPF design, we need to minimize these ripples while maintaining the necessary filtering. This can be achieved by carefully selecting the LPF's cutoff frequency, order, and type, as well as optimizing the phase shifter network in the analog beamforming scheme.

Furthermore, advanced techniques such as precoding and digital signal processing can be employed to further reduce IBI and CCI. By considering the LPF's 25 GHz stopband in the design of these techniques, we can ensure optimal interference mitigation in multi-beam satellite systems.

In conclusion, understanding the impact of the LPF's 25 GHz stopband on interference mitigation is crucial for the design of efficient and high-capacity multi-beam satellite systems.

