**Insight on V-band (57-66 GHz) vs. Optical Systems**

The V-band (57-66 GHz) offers several advantages for inter-satellite communication, particularly in terms of reduced atmospheric absorption and lower interference compared to optical systems. In orbital configurations with closer satellite spacing, V-band can provide more robust and reliable links due to its lower path loss and better penetration through atmospheric water vapor. This makes it suitable for dense constellations, such as those used in global broadband networks.

Optical systems, while offering higher data rates, are more susceptible to atmospheric turbulence and require precise alignment. For dense LEO constellations, V-band's reduced interference and simpler alignment requirements justify its priority, especially when data rate demands are moderate to high.

