PNM_Expert (to chat_manager):

 The 45° scan range in satellite communication systems, compared to broadside beams, can significantly affect link budget calculations, particularly for edge-of-coverage users. As the beam is scanned at an angle, it results in variations in gain and side-lobe levels, which can affect the signal quality.

Edge-of-coverage users are more susceptible to signal degradation due to the reduced signal strength at the receiver end. With a 45° scan range, the gain is lower compared to broadside beams, leading to a reduction in signal strength. This necessitates higher transmit power or more robust error correction to maintain the same link budget.

Moreover, the side-lobe levels are typically higher with a 45° scan range. These side lobes can cause interference with other signals, further degrading the link quality. This is particularly problematic for edge-of-coverage users, as they are already operating close to the noise floor.

In contrast, broadside beams offer higher gain and lower side-lobe levels, providing optimal performance. However, they are less flexible in terms of coverage area, limiting their application in certain scenarios.

In the next round, let's delve deeper into the strategies used to mitigate the effects of a 45° scan range on link budget calculations, focusing on techniques for optimizing gain and managing side lobes.

