1. MPLS VPNs, with the help of Multi-Protocol Label Switching (MPLS) Label Switched Paths (LSPs) and Resource Reservation Protocol with Traffic Engineering (RSVP-TE), offer precise traffic prioritization and path selection, ensuring efficient resource utilization and minimizing congestion.

2. IPsec VPNs, on the other hand, focus on secure transmission but lack detailed link status monitoring. They rely on IP routing for path selection, which might not be optimized for QoS in backhaul segments.

Both MPLS and IPsec VPNs operate on traffic aggregates and prioritization, but MPLS offers more scalable and flexible path computation due to its ability to operate independently of the link layer technology.

In terms of ensuring strict QoS requirements in internet VPN technologies, given the best-effort service paradigm, it's crucial to implement robust congestion management and prioritization mechanisms. This includes careful dimensioning of the network to provide a safe margin in network dimensioning to avoid congestion, and blocking of low priority non-critical data traffic when network congestion cannot be avoided with the current network traffic. Additionally, using hybrid networks with IPsec VPN and layer 2 technologies can be a cost-effective solution when the number of sites is not large.

In the context of converged satellite and terrestrial networks, the high fixed latency on satellite connections may require more complex path computation algorithms to ensure efficient resource utilization and QoS guarantees.

