Material properties, particularly the choice of substrate and its dielectric constant, significantly impact the trade-off between array size and radiation efficiency in SIW arrays. A substrate with a lower dielectric constant reduces the physical size required for the SIW lines, thereby minimizing insertion loss. For instance, using a substrate with a dielectric constant of 3.4 (like Rogers RO4350B) can help reduce loss compared to higher dielectric constant materials.

Designers can also employ advanced feeding topologies, such as hybrid SIW waveguide power dividers, to optimize power distribution and reduce losses. Additionally, careful layout design and the use of low-loss materials can help mitigate the effects of increased insertion loss as array size increases.

By selecting appropriate materials and optimizing the design, designers can balance array size and radiation efficiency more effectively, ensuring high gain and performance in high-gain inter-satellite antennas.

